#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119527 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "41a8466d-8bc6-5811-a557-e5a4e840f51c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4577" :question/problem "Let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. Find $z.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z=-1 \\\\ 4x-2z=1 \\\\ 3x+y-z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1x-1y+3z=-1 \\\\ 4x+0y-2z=1 \\\\ 3x+1y-1z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by finding the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0-(-2))+(-4+6)+3(4-0) \\\\  & =2+2+12 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find $D_z,$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the *third* column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0-1)+(-8-3)-(4-0) \\\\  & =-1-11-4 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{-16}{16}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "e1eb00d6-77fe-5110-8095-a54e63981c09" :question/math-academy-id "e-4579" :question/problem "Let $\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ be the solution of the system of equations below. Given that $x = 2,$ find the value of $k.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-z=-1 \\\\ kx+3y-2z=1 \\\\ 3x-y+2z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ k & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(6-2)-2(2k+6)-(-k-9) \\\\  & =4-4k-12+k+9 \\\\  & =1-3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x = \\dfrac{D_x}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(6-2)-2(2+6)-(-1-9) \\\\  & =-4-16+10 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{D_x}{D} = \\dfrac{-10}{1-3k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=2.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-10}{1-3k} & =2 \\\\ -10 & =2-6k \\\\ 6k & =12 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119591 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "33693f3b-713f-5ffc-bd4e-27ad7a1e0c7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6172" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y-6z=8 \\\\ -6x+4y+2z=3 \\\\ 5x-5y-15z=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=\\dfrac{D_x}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and $D_x=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9x+3y+(-6)z=8 \\\\ (-6)x+4y+2z=3 \\\\ 5x+(-5)y+(-15)z=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/assets/00a69fe1d48b41e376971f3669ae51668733d36dfda18c0f9cc736d5d071a441.png)\n\nNow, to construct $D_x$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14059591/assets/14e850c9af7c0b2fdd1e36299667b2cbe91c84e8bb90030b729ee23b296ad984.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc98a559-6bbf-524f-b7e1-dbd315906bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338565} {:answer/id #uuid "0f388ca8-65e7-554d-a8fd-3d3a959d188e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338566} {:answer/id #uuid "3b7c75fe-f424-5cc1-8be5-8692c0ac3e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & -7 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 11 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338567} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c3acd0-0c0f-53a7-b83b-3fd2df08623f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 14 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338568} {:answer/id #uuid "67f041c7-d7b9-5edd-bef5-c977a079635c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & -14 \\\\ -5 & 6 & -9 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1c3acd0-0c0f-53a7-b83b-3fd2df08623f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 14 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338568} :answer-field/id #uuid "6abd8c83-28a2-5047-bc01-8fad5c56653c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc681317-cba5-5425-a2bb-3eee6e432e5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139985" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-5y+7z=14 \\\\ -5x+6y+2z=9 \\\\ 3x-2y+4z=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $z=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and ${D}_{z}=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+(-5)y+7z=14 \\\\ -5x+6y+2z=9 \\\\ 3x+(-2)y+4z=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{z}$, we replace the third column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & 14 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 9 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6787759c-82ed-5aca-bccc-3902fda81eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338572} {:answer/id #uuid "4efa8416-b71b-50a0-86ec-4a680256b17c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338573} {:answer/id #uuid "e393f701-01c8-54f7-9b17-4bfda820f1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338574} {:answer/id #uuid "d63c4c9a-1efc-53c9-b1be-d9764656f395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338575} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a65bf9-700a-5380-aeb2-5693e6a143bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e393f701-01c8-54f7-9b17-4bfda820f1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338574} :answer-field/id #uuid "5115c7d5-5395-592e-82a7-d9fb6c0fe1e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec3c1c9e-2a56-5c26-b4fb-012a626f542c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139987" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z=-2 \\\\ 2x-5y+1z=-10 \\\\ 2x+y-2z=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $66,$ then $y=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+4y+2z=-2 \\\\ 2x+(-5)y+1z=-10 \\\\ 2x+1y+(-2)z=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|=66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{y},$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the second column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -10 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-10 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -10 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(20+1)+2(-4-2)+2(-2+20) \\\\  & =42-12+36 \\\\  & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{66}{66}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c6db2f6-826c-5a27-b46a-69b5ebe01437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338579} {:answer/id #uuid "6d744429-c9d6-54f9-ab40-17933949c698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338580} {:answer/id #uuid "520e2a65-54d8-50b9-97bc-488c98f2210f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338581} {:answer/id #uuid "2d40e0d5-2d12-5098-8ea5-843f2f305c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338582} {:answer/id #uuid "7e372905-bf0a-546b-a628-c090cb9970b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c6db2f6-826c-5a27-b46a-69b5ebe01437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338579} :answer-field/id #uuid "63879d92-0fd2-5899-a811-72c8353c96d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "331e1a6c-be73-51ec-ae92-9f497d84d18c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140043" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x+5y-3z=2 \\\\ 4x-5y+kz=1 \\\\ -2x+10y-5z=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that  $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $160-70k,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5 & k \\\\ -2 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}|=160-70k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & k \\\\ -2 & 10 & -5\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & k \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}|-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & k \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}|+(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(25-10k)-5(-5+2k)-3(10-10) \\\\  & =50-20k+25-10k+0 \\\\  & =75-30k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{75-30k}{160-70k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{75-30k}{160-70k} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2(75-30k) & =160-70k \\\\ 150-60k & =160-70k \\\\ 10k & =10 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120367 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ea7e94b-6323-5b86-897a-329112c25f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118678} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f39b48-69cd-5596-bcd4-cf8bdf6b1004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118679} {:answer/id #uuid "cafb42d5-80be-546b-8219-b7845f04f276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118680} {:answer/id #uuid "4eaab1bf-79cd-5fa5-9900-5401519d718c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118681} {:answer/id #uuid "6d48d72d-27ed-5313-964a-426e0e5770dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cafb42d5-80be-546b-8219-b7845f04f276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118680} :answer-field/id #uuid "d000a6eb-dcad-5232-9f34-3f7db78c59cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cf1a77a-780e-5431-8f91-cd363218bcc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-42623" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+5y-z=1 \\\\ 2x-3z=-2 \\\\ -x+y-2z=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $42,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+5y+(-1)z=1 \\\\ 2x+0y+(-3)z=-2 \\\\ (-1)x+1y+(-2)z=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|=42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}|-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(0+3)-5(4+18)-(-2-0) \\\\  & =3-110+2 \\\\  & =-105.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{-105}{42}=-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7b2eba5-9045-5153-b0a6-973bf04a61d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118690} {:answer/id #uuid "0a057c60-a666-5486-973a-3290a0248951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118691} {:answer/id #uuid "3b287f9c-1b9e-5960-b727-61218f698283" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118692} {:answer/id #uuid "3b665ed3-c9c9-5e86-bf0c-8c8dce29a829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118693} {:answer/id #uuid "54d931a4-862f-5a3a-b548-5734689967ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b665ed3-c9c9-5e86-bf0c-8c8dce29a829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118693} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f478ace-6d29-5cfa-bd3d-9523a58d8816" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f118e080-b2e1-5b06-9da5-b10b39fd54f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42671" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y+z=5 \\\\ x+y-2z=-3 \\\\ -5x+4z=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $7,$ then $y=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x+(-3)y+1z=5 \\\\ 1x+1y+(-2)z=-3 \\\\ -5x+0y+4z=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{y},$ we take the coefficients matrix, replace the second column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}| & =5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}|-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(-12-2)-5(4-10)+(-1-15) \\\\  & =-70+30-16 \\\\  & =-56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{-56}{7}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "940ce0ea-ffe0-5888-9fc1-738917606759" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}|." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338585} {:answer/id #uuid "a10f0be3-70e7-575a-abc6-284fc02f08b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-9 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338586} {:answer/id #uuid "0408ff10-b33b-50cb-bb36-e5cacb7a36c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338587} {:answer/id #uuid "44c65132-bfe3-5b21-802c-0cba380c57c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338588} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c36170-24e6-5930-90ce-9eaa76959fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a10f0be3-70e7-575a-abc6-284fc02f08b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-9 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338586} :answer-field/id #uuid "6894f02d-7a9f-5d4f-9907-1d61d53d143c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f2938e3-2c86-58a3-8014-2fd96089eb1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42681" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+5y+z=-9 \\\\ x+y-4z=-1 \\\\ -2x+8y=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and ${D}_{x}=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+5y+1z=-9 \\\\ 1x+1y+(-4)z=-1 \\\\ -2x+8y+0z=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}-9 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a2eacfc-1baf-5e98-a123-12347af9b2ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338592} {:answer/id #uuid "4a416924-9af8-5f37-bc9b-90da11af4932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338593} {:answer/id #uuid "579b35b9-c605-57e6-a7f2-e705f7af6a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338594} {:answer/id #uuid "65b6cca5-ed91-5166-8a42-78e3059d56ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338595} {:answer/id #uuid "2becf9a9-931e-5668-9667-64eb5e6890d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a416924-9af8-5f37-bc9b-90da11af4932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338593} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b2227d5-7622-5a33-a11c-b83a9dd519a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8e9092a7-94c4-59d4-8639-d2ed92a81148" :question/math-academy-id "q-42693" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y+z=1 \\\\ 4x-z=-3 \\\\ x-y+z=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and ${D}_{y}=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x+(-3)y+1z=1 \\\\ 4x+0y+(-1)z=-3 \\\\ 1x+(-1)y+1z=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$, we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d96612d7-2c0d-5c43-a025-1e514a946d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338599} {:answer/id #uuid "c9462c7a-c4e9-5fc4-bb4e-e75059cdf0e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338600} {:answer/id #uuid "db0b321a-721a-5606-94b1-03bbeb804045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338601} {:answer/id #uuid "5958a7e0-d7b8-537a-bcc4-0db66f2af9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338602} {:answer/id #uuid "e95c287f-ac22-5725-846f-cb7fb5abbd54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5958a7e0-d7b8-537a-bcc4-0db66f2af9b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338602} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff51e010-33ca-5af4-85f5-4176bdd31b6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d5aa8446-02d6-51e0-93fb-a5e6dc5a4473" :question/math-academy-id "q-43111" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-3y-z=1 \\\\ x+4y+kz=-1 \\\\ 2x+3y+4z=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that  $z=1$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $97-21k,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|=97-21k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $z$ is given by $z=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 9\\end{aligned}| & =5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}|-(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(36+3)+3(9+2)+(3-8) \\\\  & =195+33-5 \\\\  & =223.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{223}{97-21k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $z=1.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{223}{97-21k} & =1 \\\\ 223 & =97-21k \\\\ 21k & =-126 \\\\ k & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9812fe04-1415-5520-ad4e-fdc245b3a778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118786} {:answer/id #uuid "c35b2230-fda5-57d4-bb82-ce62ae9193ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118787} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a420ac-b807-52ff-a037-5ca27357c16d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118788} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4747b5-ee3b-5cc1-b285-30466c252c07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118789} {:answer/id #uuid "55cf8714-3fa0-5a67-a894-88b9930bc4c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd4747b5-ee3b-5cc1-b285-30466c252c07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118789} :answer-field/id #uuid "384e1958-c885-58f6-8644-bf675b892926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d9b84023-6745-5aae-a304-bc4f9a263b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-43113" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-y+2z=6 \\\\ 3x+2y-z=k \\\\ 4x+3y-3z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $x=1$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $-9,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ k & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =6\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6(-6+3)+(-3k+1)+2(3k-2) \\\\  & =-18-3k+1+6k-4 \\\\  & =3k-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{3k-21}{-9}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=1.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-9}{3k-21} & =1 \\\\ -9 & =3k-21 \\\\ 12 & =3k \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24af3807-ab08-5a46-927f-f3a33c0a21ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338606} {:answer/id #uuid "3fdc2439-cb49-59e4-893d-14cf84a87d4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338607} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e3b73c-8e18-55e9-8ad9-4231ca11b4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338608} {:answer/id #uuid "a5adbe48-56a3-5d8c-b6e9-20cc2aefd6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338609} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c2291e-0e81-5a67-95d4-94237f0f79de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3e3b73c-8e18-55e9-8ad9-4231ca11b4c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338608} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc9ccb96-42ef-537e-8227-5047403b336c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5084e55-f30f-50d4-ad6b-db2d733686dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-43124" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-z=1 \\\\ x-2y+2z=3 \\\\ 3x-2y+z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $5,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+1y+(-1)z=1 \\\\ 1x+(-2)y+2z=3 \\\\ 3x+(-2)y+1z=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2-(-4))-(3-4)-(-6-(-4)) \\\\  & =2+1+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{5}{5}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eefc5148-7b44-5a1e-bbfe-26f2df7966d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338613} {:answer/id #uuid "2f86cb6c-be7c-5699-ba39-6d7b61df531a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338614} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f4dad4-0378-55cb-94b0-df546f84dfba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338615} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bb7cb3-a5be-5678-8c57-a1e7d8f3577e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338616} {:answer/id #uuid "c038bd26-b6c3-5772-8b0c-463b0b7da6de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c038bd26-b6c3-5772-8b0c-463b0b7da6de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338617} :answer-field/id #uuid "f07f5d66-77d4-5dfb-a74a-6bb683f85ed0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2852179b-8314-59b5-beb0-4eb3ba216cd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-73686" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3y-2z=4 \\\\ -2x-3y=-6 \\\\ x+4z=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and ${D}_{y}=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0x+3y+(-2)z=4 \\\\ (-2)x+(-3)y+0z=-6 \\\\ 1x+0y+4z=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$, we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9c2dc67-95ca-52f2-a292-32891000b76a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5040e5a-a75f-5d55-9282-44bcd1c78587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338620} {:answer/id #uuid "64b7da7f-60e1-531d-9477-e9821079c416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338621} {:answer/id #uuid "97d6ed59-cd0a-5cb2-9754-869150ccc2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338622} {:answer/id #uuid "460b565d-c3e3-5c6e-9b7b-fc5344b2b975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338623} {:answer/id #uuid "7cad11f9-44b6-53da-aabf-8e30ce26b61f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 12 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "460b565d-c3e3-5c6e-9b7b-fc5344b2b975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338623} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eeb98cd-5458-5d84-b1ab-f0d4fb928080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfde2756-df99-506c-8f8b-640eb254c853" :question/math-academy-id "q-73688" :question/problem "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4x+6y-z=2 \\\\ 7x-8z=12 \\\\ 3x+5y+z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $z=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and ${D}_{z}=$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-4)x+6y+(-1)z=2 \\\\ 7x+0y+(-8)z=12 \\\\ 3x+5y+1z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{z}$, we replace the third column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c540ac-3cb5-5e0e-9b7b-5e2ccf1615c5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ed72d85-0558-5884-99b9-a08d1a819b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338627} {:answer/id #uuid "08380bd6-130b-5d95-ae69-849e439e212b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338628} {:answer/id #uuid "e38ffdd1-b7f6-58c9-a2d6-62b4ab549617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338629} {:answer/id #uuid "66b08a1d-1947-594e-8b71-f81a880eae34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338630} {:answer/id #uuid "0f52caf1-b1f2-5e93-8ab7-e85730d2691d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66b08a1d-1947-594e-8b71-f81a880eae34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338630} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7669c3-8989-50dc-a76c-b4ea1f273722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebeef91b-bb14-5996-92d1-28a0a60ce495" :question/math-academy-id "q-73690" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+3z=-2 \\\\ 2x-y=3 \\\\ -x-2y+z=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $-18,$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1x+1y+3z=-2 \\\\ 2x+(-1)y+0z=3 \\\\ (-1)x+(-2)y+1z=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{x}$, we replace the first column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}|+(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(-1-0)-(3-0)-(-6+6) \\\\  & =2-3-0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{-1}{-18}=\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97d91fe9-c39a-5cf1-bdd9-0ce1e60c3382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338634} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0ca57f-a015-531e-97cd-4c402a506333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338635} {:answer/id #uuid "a19474d4-3ddb-5ea5-ac74-b6e2d2ef142c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338636} {:answer/id #uuid "34400ad0-c331-5035-a94b-6b9057827fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338637} {:answer/id #uuid "335f1839-2d8f-57aa-be7c-42007a87199e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34400ad0-c331-5035-a94b-6b9057827fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338637} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a1af9e5-1ed4-5b7b-a644-c03f0bce16a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c9a3e974-f820-5185-9b0b-9dcc400af0e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-73694" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+z=3 \\\\ x-z=k \\\\ x-2y+2z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $z=2$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $-15,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|=-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $z$ is given by $z=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & k \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & k \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0+2k)-3(1-k)+3(-2-0) \\\\  & =4k-3+3k-6 \\\\  & =7k-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{7k-9}{-15}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $z=2.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7k-9}{-15} & =2 \\\\ 7k-9 & =-30 \\\\ 7k & =-21 \\\\ k & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7263e25b-117e-528c-b2ad-2624f325da35" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 3x3 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "853a81ab-d822-586d-8414-d6ae5935a83a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338641} {:answer/id #uuid "196bbe75-f665-52f6-9880-cfdee05c2629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338642} {:answer/id #uuid "afb8eeba-8974-593d-be16-f419bd6447c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338643} {:answer/id #uuid "351bf833-39b3-5a78-a846-13f0f839edae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338644} {:answer/id #uuid "77f94c55-4a88-58f3-ae11-7f9ac42741eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "351bf833-39b3-5a78-a846-13f0f839edae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338644} :answer-field/id #uuid "016b67e1-6019-5605-a354-33b891947928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72d31309-5c4e-58c9-abc0-d67229855def" :question/math-academy-id "q-73698" :question/problem "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+2z=k \\\\ x+y=-1 \\\\ y+z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $y=-5$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $1,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & k & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1-0)-k(1-0)+2(2-0) \\\\  & =-2-k+4 \\\\  & =2-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{2-k}{1}=2-k.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $y=-5.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-k & =-5 \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
